BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:INTO THE LIGHT: Light-filled aintings by Katy Boggs
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260517T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260517T170000
UID:260501S7D3OZD45keBvhDFVxWqTw
DESCRIPTION:Katy's paintings are an exploration of light—how it shapes f
 orm\, reveals color\, and defines what we see.\n\n"I am drawn to moments w
 here light is felt as much as seen: filtering through dense leaves\, tower
 ing in the crowns of trees\, glinting off cresting waves\, or softening in
 to the quiet glow of a sunset. Light is everywhere\; without it\, there is
  no seeing. Beyond its physical presence\, it also carries a deeper resona
 nce as a symbol of hope. In a time of uncertainty\, this work seeks to bri
 ng light into the image\, both visually and metaphorically."\n\nReception:
  April 25\, 4 to 6 pm.
LOCATION:Sebastopol Gallery
URL:http://sebastopolgallery.com
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Sanctuary Concerts at UU Petaluma Presents: Baroque and {{Way}} Be
 yond
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260517T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260517T180000
UID:260430dAC2j3fTsEqGAqrY2C7SzA
DESCRIPTION:On Sunday\, May 17 at 4 p.m.\, the Unitarian Universalists of 
 Petaluma’s Sanctuary Concert Series presents a classical concert entitle
 d “Baroque and Way Beyond.”\n\nThe series highlights the work of the S
 anctuary Coalition of Sonoma County\, defending our migrant neighbors from
  deportation threats.\n\nIn a wide-ranging program that ranges from Henry 
 Purcell onward through the centuries\, soprano Rachel Walters Steiner\, tr
 umpeter Daniel Norris\, and pianist Yvonne Wormer will showcase a their co
 mbined talents in various configurations. \n\nNorris is a familiar sight 
 to audiences throughout the Bay Area\, where he is active as an orchestral
  musician \\(most often with Santa Rosa Symphony\\) and trumpet soloist. A
 s a recitalist and recording artist\, his special interests include the di
 scovery and promotion of new music for trumpet\, the rediscovery of rarely
 -performed older pieces\, and trumpet music composed by women. He has perf
 ormed and recorded the world premieres of many such works.\n\nAn accomplis
 hed multi-instrumentalist\, Norris has performed professionally on a broad
  assortment of instruments including trumpet\, French horn\, flute\, didge
 ridoo\, and ukulele. He can be heard playing several of these on the sound
 tracks to the video games Sam and Max and Wallace and Gromit’s Grand Adv
 entures.\n\nNorris is a graduate of California State University\, East Bay
  \\(B.A in Music\, 2001\\) and the Peabody Conservatory of Music \\(M.M. i
 n Trumpet Performance\, 2005\\) and is a certified Suzuki Method brass ins
 tructor. He is part of the music department faculty at Sonoma State Univer
 sity and Santa Rosa Junior College. \n\nSteiner is the Cantor and Choir D
 irector of the Unitarian Universalists of Petaluma\, where she regularly l
 eads music and conducts the “Larking About” choir. A graduate of Sonom
 a State University with a degree in Music Education emphasizing Vocal Perf
 ormance\, as well as a teaching credential in music\, Steiner is pursuing 
 a Masters in Vocal Pedagogy. She has performed extensively in classical\, 
 opera\, and theater settings\, including a recent leading role in Purcell
 ’s “Dido and Aeneas.”\n\nPianist Wormer has been a collaborative pia
 nist ever since her uncle commented that “she phrases like a singer” w
 hen at a family gathering early in her music studies. Though it took a few
  attempts\, she eventually graduated from Cal State Hayward \\(now East Ba
 y\\)\, and has gone on to accompany choirs\, singers\, and instrumentalist
 s. She is particularly intuitive and this continues to be her strength. Sh
 e loves the collaborative aspect of accompanying\, and loves poetry and pr
 ose. The combination of text and music\, and the wide variety of students 
 and instructors makes her particularly well suited for her job as a staff 
 accompanist at a university. She has worked intimately in the voice depart
 ment with many many teachers and students over the past twenty-plus years.
  She is currently also the rehearsal pianist for the Sonoma Bach choir\, a
 nd the liturgical pianist at Congregation Shomrei Torah\, a progressive re
 form synagogue in Sonoma county.\n\n“We’re excited to bring a new and 
 beautiful listening room to the already rich Petaluma musical landscape\,
 ” says David Dodd\, one of the series organizers. “The church sanctuar
 y is an acoustical gem\, and we are happy to offer this space for pure mus
 ical enjoyment.” The building\, a 1901 Italian Gothic Revival gem by not
 ed Petaluma architect Brainerd Jones\, is undergoing restoration by the co
 ngregation. Dodd says that the UU congregation sees these concerts as a wa
 y to raise awareness of the need for defense of our migrant neighbors in t
 he face of ever-increasing threats of deportation. \n\nThis is an all-age
 s event\, with no alcohol served–just some snacks and drinks for a late 
 afternoon concert. A suggested donation at the door–no advance ticket sa
 les–of $20 \\(or more!\\) will go directly to the artists. The church is
  located at the corner of 5th and B Streets in downtown Petaluma. Doors op
 en at 3:30\, so plan to arrive early to get a good seat!
LOCATION:Unitarian Universalists of Petaluma
URL:https://www.facebook.com/events/1698145118290998/
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
